---
description: Ask before a commit triggers a Netlify production build
alwaysApply: true
---

`netlify.toml` has no `ignore` command, so every commit pushed to `main` runs
`npm run build` and redeploys the live site.

**Ask before committing whether the change needs a production build.** Never
decide it silently, in either direction.

Skip the build with `[skip netlify]` anywhere in the commit message:

```powershell
git commit -m "docs: add architecture document [skip netlify]"
```

What to recommend when asking:

| Change | Needs a build |
|---|---|
| `*.md`, `.cursor/rules/*`, `.gitignore` | No — none of it reaches the bundle |
| `app/`, `components/`, `lib/`, `hooks/`, `next.config.ts`, `package.json` | Yes |
| `firestore.rules`, `storage.rules` | No — Netlify never deploys these. They ship separately with `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules`, and a client that writes a new field fails silently until that runs |
| `scripts/` | No, unless it changed something `npm run build` reads |

Two things that catch people out:

- The tag has to be on the **most recent** commit of a push. Tagging it there
  covers every other commit in the same push.
- Skipping loses nothing. The next untagged commit builds and carries the
  skipped changes with it.
